A1. They tell us at the auto show that this can only be driven by a Scotchman. ~ BARGAIN
A8. As long as this is horizontal you can pass over it and depend on the uprights. ~ BRIDGE
A13. This was issued the first of the year and even now it is considered a back number. ~ LICENSE
A14. Here is where you get an earful of tooth-paste. ~ RADIO
A16. Abraham's old home town. ~ UR
A17. A kind of a hill, but don't make a mountain out of it. ~ MOLE
A18. The way people will look when they get around the corner. ~ AGED
A19. Because this has so many letters we are abbreviating it. ~ PO
A20. A healthful spring. ~ SPA
A22. This is always infers, no matter how warm the weather is. ~ DEDUCES
A24. You will need this at the zero hour. ~ FUR
A25. Do not swear at your motor when it stalls, just think back to how long this has been stalled. ~ HORSE
A27. Instead of raising this, they are now making whoopee. ~ NED
A28. What a little sunshine may do to the dark clouds. ~ MELT
A29. A bank in Cleveland that will liquidate all of its frozen assets this spring. ~ ERIE
A30. The American family still sticks to this, even when "Lux" against it. ~ BAR
A31. The man who always leads the grand march. ~ SOUSA
A32. You will get this in just a twinkling. ~ STAR
A33. A bon-mot. ~ PUN
A34. A man may make the world believe he is this, but he can never make his family believe it. ~ WONDER
A35. A man who wears a belt with suspenders. ~ PESSIMIST
A38. A big thing in some people's lives, even if it is short. ~ MOMENT
A41. What they did at the directors' meeting. ~ MET
A42. If you want this, you will have to get "long distance" ~ AFAR
A46. Did you ever see an ant elope in Africa? They call it this. ~ ELAND
A47. This is all that father could raise on his income. ~ SON
A48. The kind of backing that a good dress-shop gives to its models. ~ NUDE
A49. What the force of newspapers will do for the country. ~ EDIT
A50. One of the largest animals that can park in 3 squares. ~ GNU
A51. If you need some food for thought, here is the grub. ~ LARVA
A52. Leave it to the Scotch to have only 3 letters in their cognizance. ~ KEN
A53. Just a hunch. ~ PRESAGE
A56. In Hollywood they put this on for a close-up. ~ LID
A57. Em's half-sister. ~ EN
A58. It was graft that started this. ~ TREE
A59. The younger set make sport of this. ~ ROMP
A61. A self-starter. ~ SE
A62. Don't beat around this bush. (You might get into an awful prickle.) ~ BRIER
A63. Eddie Cantor says prosperity is just around this. (Rudy Vallee's check proves it.) ~ CROONER
A65. What King George does when he takes his daily exercises. ~ REIGNS
A66. A classy bunch. ~ SENIORS

D1. As modest as Amos is, he never does this. ~ BLUSHES
D2. The happy landing ground. ~ AIRPORT
D3. An agent for the firm of "Charity & Mercy" (abbr.). ~ RC
D4. This still holds to a gold backing. ~ GEM
D5. That positive conductor at the electric terminal. ~ ANODE
D6. A s'Pacific piece of land. ~ ISLE
D7. If you have a friend in this, help him out. ~ NEED
D8. Just a little something to pep you up. ~ BRACER
D9. Lost one's temper. ~ RAGED
D10. Two days before St. Patrick's day. ~ IDES
D11. Webster told us that this was executed, but he did not state what for. ~ DID
D12. You will need this if you are going to start golf. ~ GO
D15. The kind of board one gets at college. ~ MORTAR
D19. The speedometer his heart was set on. ~ PULSE
D21. Only opera stars who have been kind to their throats can reach for this. ~ ARIA
D23. All together, boys. ~ UNANIMOUS
D24. A pet peeve among the clans. ~ FEUD
D26. The first one to use the crawl stroke. ~ SERPENT
D28. Out where the U. S. parks among the Rockies. ~ MONTANA
D30. The Colossus of Roads. ~ BUS
D31. Help wanted. ~ SOS
D33. An interjection that demands attention. ~ PST
D34. There is something funny about this. ~ WIT
D36. Here is the conclusion we have reached. ~ END
D37. "Woman's Home Companion" (several copies). ~ MEN
D38. What his wife has made him. ~ MEEKER
D39. This is always opposed to anything modern. ~ OLDEN
D40. The spring that always comes on time. ~ MAIN
D43. What sailors do to roll their own. ~ FURL
D44. A swanky name for a back-seat driver. ~ ADVISER
D45. The publisher's audience. ~ READERS
D47. Hateful expressions. ~ SNEERS
D50. The shade of the old apple tree. ~ GREEN
D51. Soury, but this is the only flavor we have left. ~ LEMON
D53. What teacher's pet was to the rest of the class. ~ PRIG
D54. What those enticing rainbows are. ~ ARCS
D55. What the bulls do when things are down. ~ GORE
D58. One of the angles of matrimony. ~ TRI
D60. A mouthful from Hawaii. ~ POI
D62. This is to continue in existence and its future depends on a will. ~ BE
D64. A sharp negative. ~ NO


